chat_messages.text:
 Your console logs are showing `body` and `title` as undefined because you're missing the body parser middleware. Express doesn't automatically parse JSON request bodies - you need to explicitly add middleware to handle this.

The issue is that when your logging middleware runs, the request body hasn't been parsed yet, so `req.body` is still undefined. Additionally, `req.title` isn't a standard Express property - it looks like you're expecting the title from the JSON body, which wouldn't be directly accessible as `req.title` anyway.

Here's how to fix it:

1. Add the Express JSON body parser middleware **before** your logging middleware:

```javascript
// Add this BEFORE your logging middleware
app.use(express.json());

// Then your logging middleware
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  // Your existing logging code...
});
```

2. Fix the title logging (title is inside the body, not directly on the request):

```javascript
console.log('Request Body:', req.body);
console.log('Request Title:', req.body?.title); // Use optional chaining
```

The order of middleware in Express is very important. The `express.json()` middleware needs to run first to parse the JSON request body before your logging middleware tries to access `req.body`.

Also, for large request bodies, you might want to conditionally log or truncate the content to avoid filling your logs with too much data:

```javascript
console.log('Request Body:', 
  req.body ? JSON.stringify(req.body).substring(0, 1000) + (JSON.stringify(req.body).length > 1000 ? '...' : '') : undefined);
```

Make these changes and your logging should work correctly.

